{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Athens homes have a downspout problem they don't even know about. Historic homes around Athens Square often have original downspouts that are undersized by modern standards — they were installed when rainfall expectations and building codes were different. Newer construction frequently uses builder-grade downspouts that meet minimum code but can't handle intense storms.</p>

<p>The consequences become apparent during heavy rains. Undersized downspouts can't keep up with the volume, causing gutters to back up and overflow. Even worse, many downspouts simply dump water right next to the foundation — a few inches from your home at best.</p>

<p>Limestone County's soil conditions affect how this plays out. Water that pools near your foundation may not drain away quickly, especially in areas with heavier soil composition. Over time, this leads to foundation problems, basement moisture, and landscape erosion.</p>

<p>The fix is usually straightforward: properly sized downspouts, correctly positioned, with extensions that move water well away from your foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and local rainfall. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows to corners and low points in your gutter system. Poorly placed downspouts create dead zones where water pools.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For comprehensive protection, underground drains route water 10+ feet away — and they're invisible once installed.</p>
{% endset %}

